# 4. Storage Lens vs CloudWatch

This is an important interview distinction.

### CloudWatch

Primarily:

> **Monitor AWS resources and applications.**

Example:

```text
EC2 CPU
Lambda errors
ALB requests
RDS metrics
S3 service metrics
```

---

### S3 Storage Lens

Primarily:

> **Analyze and understand your S3 storage usage and activity across buckets/accounts/Regions.**

Think:

```text
CloudWatch
    ↓
General AWS monitoring

Storage Lens
    ↓
S3 storage visibility & analytics
```

---

# 5. Storage Lens vs S3 Inventory

Another common interview question.

### Storage Lens

Answers:

> **"How is my S3 storage environment behaving?"**

Example:

```text
How much storage do I have?
Which buckets are growing?
Which storage classes are being used?
How many objects do I have?
```

### S3 Inventory

Answers:

> **"What objects actually exist in my bucket?"**

For example:

```text
bucket:
company-data

Inventory:
file1.pdf
file2.jpg
backup.zip
log1.txt
...
```

It can provide object-level information such as:

```text
Object key
Size
Storage class
Encryption status
ETag
Version ID
```

So remember:

```text
Storage Lens
    ↓
BIG PICTURE / ANALYTICS

S3 Inventory
    ↓
OBJECT-LEVEL INVENTORY
```

---

# 6. Storage Lens vs S3 Analytics

Don't confuse them either.

A simple way to remember:

```text
S3 Storage Lens
       ↓
Understand S3 usage across your environment

S3 Inventory
       ↓
List and report on objects

S3 Storage Class Analysis
       ↓
Understand access patterns to help choose
storage classes
```

